SITE     name="Flint"  nav="Docs,Cheatsheet,Licence"  location="Open Source"  contact="flint@opensource.org"
THEME    family=technical  accent=#0f4c75  mode=light  density=tight
SECTION  id=s1  kind=hero  layout=split  tone=quiet
TEXT     sec=s1  slot=eyebrow  text="Flint"
TEXT     sec=s1  slot=title    text="Find [unused] indexes in Postgres"
TEXT     sec=s1  slot=lede     text="A read-only CLI that scans your database schema and reports dead indexes. It runs silently inside CI pipelines to keep queries fast."
ACTION   sec=s1  slot=primary  label="Read the docs"    href="#docs"
ACTION   sec=s1  slot=secondary label="View on GitHub"     href="#"
MEDIA    sec=s1  slot=figure  motif=schematic
SECTION  id=s2  kind=features  layout=list  tone=quiet
TEXT     sec=s2  slot=eyebrow    text="Find [unused] indexes"
TEXT     sec=s2  slot=title      text="Dead code that slows down your queries."
ITEM     sec=s2  slot=item       title="Index scan detection" body="Flint parses every table definition and cross-references it against the query log to identify which indexes are never touched. It runs in under two minutes on a schema with ten thousand tables, reporting dead index names directly into your CI pipeline."
ITEM     sec=s2  slot=item       title="Query performance impact" body="A single unused index can add fifteen milliseconds per scan across millions of rows; Flint catches this before production traffic hits it. The tool flags any table where the optimizer chooses a sequential scan instead of an index lookup, saving you from silent slowdowns."
ITEM     sec=s2  slot=item       title="CI integration" body="Flint hooks into your build script on every commit and exits with code one if dead indexes are found, blocking merges until they're addressed. It requires no write access to the database, scanning only read-only views of the schema metadata."
ITEM     sec=s2  slot=item       title="Maintenance cost reduction" body="Automating this check prevents index bloat that grows over months and wastes storage on tables with zero usage patterns. The tool generates a remediation plan suggesting which indexes can be dropped or replaced without breaking application logic."
SECTION  id=s3  kind=detail  layout=split  tone=quiet
TEXT     sec=s3  slot=eyebrow    text="Flint"
TEXT     sec=s3  slot=title      text="Why [Postgres] can't see what you need."
TEXT     sec=s3  slot=lede     text="The database optimizer is blind to your application's actual query patterns. It trusts the schema, not the code that runs against it, leading to indexes built for ghosts and queries that stall in production. Only Flint bridges this gap by correlating live execution traces with static definitions."
ACTION   sec=s3  slot=primary  label="See a demo"    href="#demo"
MEDIA    sec=s3  slot=figure  motif=dial
SECTION  id=s4  kind=faq  layout=stack  tone=bold
TEXT     sec=s4  slot=eyebrow    text="Flint"
TEXT     sec=s4  slot=title      text="Why [Postgres] can't see what you need."
ITEM     sec=s4  slot=item       title="How does Flint actually find dead indexes?" body="The tool parses your schema definitions and cross-references them against a live query log to identify which indexes are never touched. It runs in under two minutes on schemas with ten thousand tables, reporting dead index names directly into your CI pipeline."
ITEM     sec=s4  slot=item       title="Does Flint require write access?" body="No, it requires no write access to the database and scans only read-only views of the schema metadata. This means you can run it safely in production environments without risking data modification or triggering security alerts."
ITEM     sec=s4  slot=item       title="How often should I run this check?" body="Run Flint on every commit during your build pipeline so dead indexes are caught before they impact performance. The tool exits with code one if issues are found, blocking merges until the team addresses them."
ITEM     sec=s4  slot=item       title="What happens to my existing queries after cleanup?" body="Flint generates a remediation plan suggesting which indexes can be dropped or replaced without breaking application logic. You apply these changes during maintenance windows and see immediate improvements in query speed."
ITEM     sec=s4  slot=item       title="Is there any cost beyond the tool itself?" body="There is no licensing fee since Flint is open source under MIT, but you will need to allocate compute time for schema parsing and log analysis. The entire scan completes within minutes on standard server hardware."
SECTION  id=s5  kind=roster  layout=stack  tone=quiet
TEXT     sec=s5  slot=eyebrow    text="Flint"
TEXT     sec=s5  slot=title      text="Built by [engineers]"
ITEM     sec=s5  slot=item       title="Core developers" body="Maintains the parser and CI hooks for Postgres schemas, ensuring compatibility with modern database versions."
ITEM     sec=s5  slot=item       title="Open source maintainers" body="Contributes to documentation and community support channels under MIT license terms."
ITEM     sec=s5  slot=item       title="Performance reviewers" body="Validates query logs against schema definitions in production environments, focusing on zero-write access safety."
SECTION  id=s6  kind=cta  layout=stack  tone=quiet
TEXT     sec=s6  slot=title    text="What comes next"
TEXT     sec=s6  slot=lede      text="When dead indexes are removed, query plans tighten and latency drops by fifteen percent on average."
ACTION   sec=s6  slot=primary  label="Get started now"  href="#install"
